# HQL-0064: `parallel`

Companion to [[HQL-0064]]. HQL evaluates the statements of a program in
order, one after another. A program that writes to a child while it reads
the child's answers needs two statements to run at once, and HQL has no
way to write that. This note defines the keyword `parallel`, which states
that its branches run at the same time and joins them.

!!! warning "Design only"
    Nothing in this note is implemented, and [[HQL-0064]] does not deploy
    it. The syntax is a sketch for discussion.

!!! abstract "TL;DR"
    - **Statements run in order.** `parallel` is the one construct that
      runs two of them at once.
    - **`parallel { a = …, b = … }` runs named branches at the same time**
      and gives the record of their results, as `fork` does. The block
      ends when every branch has ended: that is the join.
    - **`xs | parallel(f, jobs = 4)` runs one function over many
      elements**, at most `jobs` at once, and keeps the input's order. It
      is GNU `parallel` and `xargs -P` as a pipeline step.
    - **`parallel(join = any) { … }` ends at the first branch that ends**
      and cancels the others.
    - **What runs at once is syntax. How it runs is host configuration**:
      tasks, threads or processes, chosen in `[execution]`.
    - **Pure steps need no keyword.** The planner already may run them in
      parallel ([[pipes]]).

## What runs at once today

| | Runs at the same time | Written as |
|---|---|---|
| two statements | never | |
| the branches of `fork` | no: each branch is a pipeline over one input | `xs \| fork { a = …, b = … }` |
| adjacent pure elementwise steps | when the backend splits work | nothing: the planner decides |
| two children joined by one pipeline | yes: they are two processes | `tap(a, F) \| sink(b, F)` |
| a child and the pipeline that reads it | yes | `tap(c, F) \| …` |

The last two rows are the only parallelism a script has today, and both
are inside one pipeline. Everything in this note is about the first row.

## The block: `parallel { … }`

```hql
r = parallel {
    commits = source(command("git", ["log", "--oneline"]), Lines)
    issues  = source(http("https://example.org/issues.json"), Json)
}
{commits: size(r.commits), issues: size(r.issues)}
```

The syntax is `fork`'s, without an input:

```text
parallel [ "(" option { "," option } ")" ] "{" branch { separator branch } "}"
branch    = name "=" expression
separator = line break | ","
option    = name "=" expression
```

- Each branch is an expression. A branch continues over lines that open
  with `|`, as a branch of `fork` does.
- `hql` MUST start every branch before it waits for any.
- The block MUST end when every branch has ended. Its value is the record
  of the branches' results, `{a: A, b: B}`, each field typed by its own
  branch. `{ a, b } = parallel { … }` binds the branches by name.
- A branch whose result is a `Stream<T>` MUST be read to its end inside
  the branch, and its field is a `List<T>`. The join is the point where a
  stream becomes a batch.
- The statement after the block MUST run after the block has ended.

### What a branch may read

- A branch MAY read any binding made before the block. A value is
  immutable, so two branches that read one binding do not interfere.
- A branch MUST NOT read a binding another branch of the same block makes.
  It is a check error that names both branches.
- Two branches communicate through an endpoint: a `Channel<T>`, a child's
  pipes or a named pipe.

### Effects

`parallel` changes one rule of [[HQL-0055]] and no other.

- Effects of two branches MUST be treated as unordered. The program states
  that by writing `parallel`.
- Effects within one branch MUST keep their order.
- Two branches that write one channel append in the order their writes
  reach it.
- A `parallel` block inside a `with transaction` scope MUST be refused. The
  writes of one transaction are ordered.
- A child's lifetime is bounded by the branch that started it: when the
  branch ends, `hql` terminates a child it left running
  ([child processes](02-child-processes.hmd)).

### Failure

- An `err` is a value. A branch whose result is `err(e)` has ended, and
  its field holds the `err`. No other branch is affected.
- An unrecoverable failure in one branch MUST cancel every other branch of
  the block, and the block fails with it.
- **Cancelling** a branch stops its evaluation at its next read or write
  and terminates the children it started.

## The step: `xs | parallel(f, jobs = n)`

GNU `parallel` and `xargs -P` run one command for each input line, several
at a time:

```sh
ls *.log | parallel -j 4 gzip {}
```

The step form is the same operation over values:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.fs import *
from io.process import *

folder(working_dir()) | glob(Glob("*.log"))
| parallel(e => command("gzip", [e.name]).run(), jobs = 4)
```

```hql
/// `f` of each element, at most `jobs` at the same time. The results are
/// in the order of the input.
effectful fn parallel<T, U>(self: Seq<T>, f: T -> U, *, jobs: Int = 4) : List<U>
```

| GNU `parallel` | HQL |
|---|---|
| `-j 4` | `jobs = 4` |
| `-k`, keep the input's order | always |
| `{}`, the argument | the parameter of `f` |
| `--joblog`, the status of each job | the result: `List<Result<Output, CommandFailure>>` |
| `--timeout` | `command(…, timeout_ms = …)` |
| `--halt now,fail=1` | an unrecoverable failure cancels the rest |

- `parallel(f)` MUST give the results in the order of the input, whatever
  order the calls end in.
- At most `jobs` calls of `f` MUST be running at one time.
- `f` MAY be effectful. Effects of two calls are unordered, as effects of
  two branches are.
- A pure `f` gives the value `map(f)` gives. `map` is then the step to
  write, and the planner decides whether it runs in parallel.
- The failure rules of the block apply, each call being one branch.

## The first to end: `join = any`

`join` states when the block ends. `all`, the default, waits for every
branch. `any` waits for the first.

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

// Follow the log until SIGTERM arrives.
parallel(join = any) {
    lines = tap(file("app.log").end, Lines) | sink(stdout, Text)
    stop  = signals(Signal("TERM")) | take(1)
}
```

- With `join = any`, the block MUST end when one branch ends, and `hql`
  MUST cancel the others.
- Each field is then an `Option<T>`: the result of a branch that ended,
  and `none` for one that was cancelled.

This is the step the companion note [`io.process`](01-io-process.hmd)
found missing: one stream ends another.

## A conversation

The conversation of the companion note
[advanced child processes](03-advanced-child-processes.hmd) is two
branches, one that reads the child and one that writes it:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

questions = Channel<String>("questions")
bc = command("bc", ["-l"], stdin_from = questions)

r = parallel {
    answers = tap(bc, Lines) | take(2)
    asked   = ["2 + 2", "10 / 4"] | sink(questions)
}
r.answers
```

```text
["4", "2.50000000000000000000"]
```

```d2
direction: down

before: "statements before the block\nrun in order"
start: "parallel {\nevery branch starts"
a: "branch answers\ntap(bc) | take(2)"
b: "branch asked\nsink(questions)"
join: "}\nevery branch has ended"
after: "the statement after the block\nreads r.answers"

before -> start
start -> a
start -> b
a -> join
b -> join
join -> after
```

## Branches, children and the one parent

A branch and a child are different things, and neither is a Unix `fork`
of the program.

| | What it is | Started by | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| the `hql` process | the one process that evaluates the program | the user, or `#!` | |
| a branch | a unit of evaluation inside the `hql` process | `parallel` | light: no new process under `tasks` or `threads` |
| a child | another program, a process of its own | `source`, `tap`, `target`, `sink` or `run` of a `Command` | heavy: a process, with its own memory |

- The program is never duplicated. A child runs the program a `Command`
  names, started by the runtime with the kernel's spawn operation. HQL's
  `fork` is a fan-out of one value to several pipelines ([[pipes]]), not
  the system call.
- `hql` MUST be the parent of every child, whichever branch started it.
  A branch does not own a process; it waits on the pipes of one.
- `hql` supervises its children: it reads their pipes, collects their exit
  statuses, and terminates one whose branch has ended. That supervision
  tests only that a child exists and has ended. A question about whether a
  child still does its work is a watchdog's
  ([watchdogs](06-watchdog.hmd)).

```d2
direction: down

hql: "hql process\nthe one parent" {
  direction: down
  a: "branch report\nwaits on the child's stdout"
  b: "branch sent\nwrites the named pipe"
}
fifo: "named pipe\na kernel object at a path"
child: "child process\nanalyse --input /tmp/frames"

hql.b -> fifo: "frames"
fifo -> child: "frames"
child -> hql.a: "report, on stdout"
```

The diagram is the example "A named pipe and a child" of the companion
note [named pipes](04-named-pipes.hmd): two light branches in one process,
and one heavy child beside it.

## How it runs

What runs at the same time changes what a program does, so it is syntax.
How the branches are run changes only how fast, so it is host
configuration, as the choice of backend is ([[pipes]]):

```toml
[execution]
parallel = "tasks"       # tasks | threads | processes
```

| `parallel` | A branch is | Suited to | Limit |
|---|---|---|---|
| `tasks` | a task of the runtime, many on one thread | branches that wait: children, sockets, pipes | a branch that computes holds the others up |
| `threads` | an operating-system thread | branches that compute | one thread for each running branch |
| `processes` | a worker `hql` process | isolation: a branch that crashes does not end the others | values cross by serialization, and a `Channel` is not shared |

- `tasks` MUST be the default. A script's branches mostly wait for a child
  or an endpoint.
- Every backend MUST run the branches of a block at the same time. A
  conversation with a child does not end otherwise, so this is not an
  optimization a backend may skip. A backend that cannot MUST refuse the
  program.
- The value of a block whose branches do not communicate MUST NOT depend
  on the setting.
- Under `processes`, a branch that reads or writes a `Channel` MUST be
  refused.

The reference implementation has the pieces in Rust: `tokio`, already an
optional dependency of the crate, for `tasks` and for child processes;
`std::thread::scope` or `rayon` for `threads`; and worker processes
started as children for `processes`.

## Backwards Compatibility

`parallel` becomes a reserved word. A program that binds or calls the name
`parallel` no longer parses.
